# 🎛️ Fine-tuning a 450M vision model on this laptop

The [visual control probe](visual-control.md) showed a small vision model can act on what it
sees: LFM3-VL-2B gets 144 of 150 one-picture decisions right with thinking on, and 117 without
it. Can a model a quarter of that size learn the same job in a few minutes of training on an
8 GB GPU? And how much of the model do we actually have to train?

Everything is in [`bench/finetune/`](../bench/finetune/): `train_glance.py` (fit test, training,
evaluation) and the results. Every run on this page has its commands in
[Reproducing our results](reproduce.md).

## The tiny methods vs LoRA, in short

| | Norm-only (BN, LN, RMSNorm gains) | IA3 | LoRA |
|---|---|---|---|
| What it trains | the gains already in the norm layers | one new scale per row or column of a layer | a small low-rank matrix added to a layer's weights |
| What that can do | turn existing features up or down | turn existing features up or down | build new mixes of features |
| Size | smallest | small | biggest of the three |
| Good for | changing behaviour the model already has in it | the same | adding things the model doesn't know |
| On disk | [baked into a whole model](#serving-it-through-llamacpp) | baked into a whole model | a separate file you can dial, switch off or stack |

Think of a mixing desk. Norm-only and IA3 only move the faders, so if the sound you want is already
on a track, that's all you need, and it's why they did so well here: LFM3-VL could already see the
scenes and only had to change how it acted on them. LoRA adds new tracks. That costs more, but it's
what you need for knowledge the model doesn't have, like one person's face.

None of this is new. Style carried in norm parameters goes back to
[Dumoulin et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07629) and [AdaIN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06868)
(Huang & Belongie). [Training only BatchNorm](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00152) is from Frankle et
al., [LayerNorm tuning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11420) for language models from Zhao et al., and
[IA3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05638) from Liu et al.'s T-Few. Variants of LoRA exist (DoRA, OFT,
LoKr), but a [2026 study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22708) reports that a well-tuned plain LoRA
matches or beats most of them; we've only seen its summary, not read it through.

## The setup

- Model: [LFM2.5-VL-450M](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM2.5-VL-450M) (Liquid AI, LFM
  Open License v1.0), loaded in fp16 with plain transformers 5.22: native support, **no
  `trust_remote_code`**, weights pinned and checked by sha256. [LFM3-VL-2B](models/lfm3-vl-2b.md)
  was the first choice, but transformers has no LFM3 support yet and its repo's own Python has to
  run, so it came later, in a sandbox ([below](#lfm3-vl-2b-in-a-sandbox)).
- Task: the five glance scenes (tanks, a dialog's buttons, objects on a table, a traffic
  light, a pressure gauge). Training uses 400 scenes of each kind from seeds the test never uses
  (`train-tanks-17`), so no test picture is ever trained on.
- Target: the right tool call, rendered through the model's own chat template
  (`<|tool_call_start|>[refill(tank="B")]<|tool_call_end|>`), or a one-line "no such button"
  when no call is right. The loss covers only the answer.
- Training: one pass over 2,000 scenes, batch 8 (gradient accumulation), fp16 autocast with
  fp32 copies of what is trained (the RTX 2070 has no bf16), gradient checkpointing.
- Scoring: the probe's own 30 scenes per kind and the probe's own scorers. The answers are
  parsed with Python's `ast` (call names and literal arguments only); nothing the model writes is
  run.

## The results

| What we trained | Trainable parameters | Training time | Peak VRAM | 🛢️ | 🖱️ | 🤏 | 🚦 | ⏲️ | **All** (clean) | Degraded pictures |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| nothing (the base model) | 0 | — | — | 8 | 6 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **14** | 19 |
| **LoRA** (rank 16, attention + MLP + conv projections), 3 seeds | 6.0M (1.3%) | 7.5 min | 1.36 GB | 30 / 30 / 26 | 24 / 28 / 21 | 22 / 19 / 22 | 30 ×3 | 30 ×3 | **136 / 137 / 129**, mean 134 | 118 / 130 / 118, mean **122** |
| **Norm-only**, language model (its RMSNorm gains), 3 seeds | **34,560 (0.008%)** | 5.6 min | 1.27 GB | 28 / 30 / 29 | 25 / 21 / 23 | 22 / 23 / 22 | 30 ×3 | 30 / 26 / 29 | **135 / 130 / 133**, mean 133 | 121 / 116 / 115, mean 117 |
| **IA3** (a learned scale per channel), lr 1e-2, 3 seeds | 79,872 (0.018%) | 5.8 min | 1.27 GB | 27 / 25 / 18 | 29 / 25 / 22 | 25 / 21 / 20 | 30 / 30 / 30 | 27 / 21 / 19 | **138 / 122 / 109**, mean 123 | 133 / 108 / 92, mean 111 |
| IA3, lr 3e-3 | 79,872 | 5.8 min | 1.27 GB | 24 | 24 | 22 | 30 | 17 | 117 | 102 |
| **Norm-only, vision side** (the image encoder's LayerNorms) | 38,400 (0.009%) | 5.5 min | 1.27 GB | 15 | 0 | 0 | 30 | 28 | 73 | 45 |
| *for comparison:* [LFM3-VL-2B](models/lfm3-vl-2b.md), no training, thinking on / off | — | — | — | 25 / 24 | 30 / 30 | 29 / 26 | 30 / 27 | 30 / 10 | *144 / 117* | — |

Out of 150 (30 scenes of each kind). Measured 2026-10-29, greedy decoding in transformers, under
0.25 s a decision. "Degraded pictures" are the same test scenes shrunk to 70%, darker or
brighter, lower contrast and JPEG-compressed at quality 35, a worse camera. LoRA, the
language-model norms and IA3 at 1e-2 were trained three times (seeds 0, 1, 2: a different order of the training
scenes and a different start for LoRA and IA3); the others once. LFM3-VL's row was measured through
llama.cpp with a forced call, so the harnesses differ.

## What we learned

- The base model never calls a tool with a picture in front of it. Its card says function
  calling works "for text-only input", and here it held: 0 calls in 150 scenes. It answered in
  prose ("I'm sorry, I don't have the capability to control a robot arm"), wrote the tool's
  name as plain text (`stop`, `close_valve`), and misread the tanks ("A: 50% (below 30%)"). Its
  14 points are all scenes where *no* call was the right answer.
- A few minutes of training fixes that, and more. Around 134/150 after LoRA: well above
  LFM3-VL-2B without thinking (117), close to it with thinking (144), at a quarter of its size
  and without a 3-second think. Every answer is now a well-formed call, multiple tank refills
  included. What's left is mostly clicks and picks a little off target.
- Training only the language model's norm gains is as good as LoRA on clean pictures (mean
  133 against 134, and the seeds overlap: 130–135 against 129–137) with 173× fewer trainable
  numbers. On degraded pictures LoRA keeps a little more (mean 122 against 117). Our first,
  single-seed run said the opposite (121 against 118): one run was not enough to tell them apart.
  So the "tune only the normalization" idea works on a transformer for teaching it *how to
  answer* in a narrow domain; it just isn't more robust than LoRA.
- IA3 is the least predictable. At 3e-3 it lagged (117, calling `close_valve` on 13 dials
  where something else was right). At 1e-2 its first run was the best of all (138 clean, 133
  degraded), and the next two seeds gave 122 and 109: a mean of 123, with the widest spread.
  LoRA and the norms are steadier.
- The vision side alone teaches the format, not the pointing. Training only the image
  encoder's LayerNorms made the model *call* tools (traffic light 30/30, gauge 28/30), so a
  nudge in what the language model sees is enough to switch it into tool-calling mode. But its
  clicks and picks are placeholders, the same (500, 500) or (400, 400) whatever the picture
  (0/30 each), and it refills all three tanks. Where to point is decided in the language model.
- LoRA's misses on degraded pictures look like over-learning the clean ones: in its first
  run it refilled tanks that aren't low, often all three, and the tank score fell from 30 to 11.
- The caveat: training and test pictures come from the same drawing code, with different
  seeds. This shows the model can learn *our* scenes, not that it learned gauges in general. The
  degraded-picture test is a first step; real photos would be the real test.

## The grid robot: from 0 to 5 of 8 mazes

The same recipe on the probe's [grid task](visual-control.md#the-tests): a 7×7 grid, a blue
robot, a green goal, and in half the episodes 11 wall cells. One fresh request per step (the
picture, the rules and the last three moves as text), one `move` call. Training data: every step
of the shortest walk through 400 grids from their own seeds (2,756 steps, no test grid among
them), the target being a move on a shortest path. Test: the probe's own 16 grids, closed loop.

| Model | Open grids | Mazes | Steps / shortest (solved) | Blocked moves | Time per step |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| LFM2.5-VL-450M, base | 0/8 | 0/8 | — | — | — |
| LFM2.5-VL-450M + LoRA (9.9 min of training) | **8/8** | 1/8 | 1.00 | 154 | ~0.15 s |
| LFM2.5-VL-450M + norm-only (7.1 min) | 7/8 | **3/8** | 1.00 open, 1.24 mazes | 95 | ~0.15 s |
| LFM2.5-VL-450M + LoRA, **harness skips moves seen blocked** | **8/8** | **5/8** | 1.00 open, 1.35 mazes | 14 | ~0.2 s |
| LFM2.5-VL-450M + norm-only, **harness skips moves seen blocked** | 7/8 | **5/8** | 1.00 open, 1.58 mazes | 16 | ~0.2 s |
| *controls:* base + the same harness / a random walk + the same harness (mean of 200) | *0/8 / 0.7* | *0/8 / 0.4* | — | — | — |
| *[LFM3-VL-2B](models/lfm3-vl-2b.md), thinking, no training* | *8/8* | *3/8* | *1.50* | *6* | *44 s* |
| *[Qwen3-VL-8B](models/qwen3-vl-8b.md), no training* | *8/8* | *1/8* | *1.80* | *67* | *0.7 s* |

On open grids both tuned models walk the shortest path every time they arrive. In mazes they do
what the thinking model does in a few hundred times the time, but they fail differently: the
thinking model rarely bumps a wall and runs out of steps thinking, the tuned ones walk straight
into a wall and keep pressing (one norm-only episode: 28 blocked moves out of 28). They learned
"head for the goal", not "go around": the training walks never show a blocked move, so the
"(blocked)" in the prompt means nothing to them. One seed each.

**Teaching recovery didn't work; a harness rule did.** Adding 4 random off-path states per grid
to the training data, most of them right after a bump ("left (blocked)" in the prompt), made both
models *worse* (LoRA 6/8 and 1/8, norm-only 3/8 and 1/8). In hindsight that data couldn't teach
what we wanted: the shortest-path move from a cell is the same whether or not you just bumped, so
"(blocked)" never changed the right answer and there was nothing to learn from it. The real
failure is one wrong move at one cell: after a bump the picture doesn't change, so a model that
only looks at the picture makes the same wrong move forever. So we moved the fix into the
harness: score all four moves by the model's own probability and take the best one that hasn't
already been blocked from this cell. Both tuned models then solve **5 of 8 mazes**, ahead of
LFM3-VL-2B with thinking (3/8) at a fraction of a second per step. The controls show the harness
isn't doing the work: with the base model it solves nothing, and a random walk under the same
rule averages 0.4 of 8. That's the visual control lesson again: let the harness keep the state
and the rules, and ask the model only what it's good at.

## One adapter for both jobs

Trained on the glance scenes and the grid walks together (4,756 examples, one pass):

| Trained on both | Glance, clean | Glance, degraded | Grid open / mazes | Grid open / mazes, harness skips blocked moves | Training |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| LoRA, 1 seed | 127 | 124 | 8/8, 0/8 | 8/8, 5/8 | 17 min |
| **Norm-only** (34,560 parameters), 3 seeds | 139 / 115 / 133, mean 129 | 118 / 102 / 135, mean 118 | 8/8 ×3; 0, 1, 2 /8 | **8/8 and 6/8, all three seeds** | 13 min |

**Steering is the solid part:** all three norm-only seeds walk every open grid and, with the
harness rule, solve 6 of 8 mazes (LFM3-VL-2B with thinking:
3/8; the tuned grid-only models: 5/8; tuned LFM3-VL's IA3 later reached
[7/8](#did-it-hold-up-2026-10-30)). **The glance side gets less steady when it shares the
model:** the three seeds score 139, 115 and 133. The 115 comes from one habit: that model calls
`report` on 20 of 30 gauges, including the ones that needed a valve, the very mistake
LFM3-VL-2B makes without thinking. So the same 34,560 numbers can hold both behaviours, but
which rule survives training is a matter of luck at this size of run. Train each job's adapter
separately, or check every run, before trusting one.

## Serving it through llama.cpp

LFM2.5 and LFM3-VL both run tuned in `llama-server` now. A LoRA converts to an adapter GGUF
(tried on LFM3-VL only). Norm-only and IA3 have no adapter format, but they don't need one:
[`bake_gguf.sh`](../bench/finetune/bake_gguf.sh) writes them into a normal GGUF. Norm-only replaces
the gains. IA3 multiplies each layer's weight by its scales, along the rows for an output scale and
the columns for an input one, which is the same sum the live scale does. It's plain arithmetic on
`model.safetensors`, so none of the model repo's code runs and no sandbox is needed.

    bash bench/finetune/bake_gguf.sh models/lfm3-vl-2b-hf models/lfm3-vl-2b-adapters/ia3 models/lfm3-vl-2b-baked/ia3.gguf Q8_0
    QMODEL=lfm3vl MODEL=models/lfm3-vl-2b-baked/ia3.gguf bash scripts/run-qwen.sh --image

A baked file is a whole model (1.8 GB for LFM3-VL), not a small add-on, and you can't dial it with
`/lora-adapters`. Run with no adapter, the pipeline rebuilt both official GGUFs tensor for tensor,
byte-identical; baking then changed exactly the tensors it should (119 and 46 on LFM3-VL), and
`audit-gguf` came back clean.

Scores out of 150, clean / never-seen scenes / degraded pictures, thinking off:

| Model | Adapter | In `llama-server` | In transformers |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| LFM3-VL-2B Q8_0 | none | 109 / 110 / 109 | 97 / 105 / — |
| | norm-only | **150 / 149 / 150** | 150 / 149 / 150 |
| | IA3 | **150 / 148 / 149** | 150 / 147 / 149 |
| | LoRA (`--lora`) | 149 / — / — | 149 / 150 / 149 |
| LFM2.5-VL-450M F16 | none | 14 / 14 / 17 | 14 / 14 / 19 |
| | norm-only | 133 / 77 / 121 | 135 / — / 121 |
| | IA3 | 139 / 111 / 134 | 138 / — / 133 |
| | norm-only, glance + grid | 138 / 101 / 118 | 139 / 98 / 118 |

So nothing is lost in the move, and tuned LFM3-VL decides in 0.3 s in 3.1 GB. LFM2.5's norm-only
adapter barely generalizes (77), which the transformers runs never tested; its IA3 does best (111).
LFM2.5 served at 1.5 GB and 0.1 s a decision. The untuned LFM3-VL gained 12 points in the server,
which uses its own sampling, not greedy decoding.

Tools used to be the blocker for LFM2.5: any tool with a string argument failed with "failed to
parse grammar". llama.cpp looks for the text `List of tools: [`, but the VL template writes
`"List of tools: " + (tools | tojson)`, so it fell through to a generic analyzer that built a broken
grammar. One-line fix on our fork (`3cb2992`). Start it with `QMODEL=lfm25vl MODEL=<gguf> bash
scripts/run-qwen.sh --image`, which uses the sampling it was tested with, the same as LFM3-VL's.

## Run it yourself

```bash
mamba create -n vlft python=3.12 pip
mamba activate vlft
pip install --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu126 -r envs/vlft.txt
bash scripts/download-hf-weights.sh lfm25vl
python bench/finetune/train_glance.py fit   --model models/lfm2.5-vl-450m-hf          # one step, peak VRAM
python bench/finetune/train_glance.py train --model models/lfm2.5-vl-450m-hf --out models/lfm2.5-vl-450m-adapters/norm --method norm --lr 1e-3
python bench/finetune/train_glance.py eval  --model models/lfm2.5-vl-450m-hf --adapter models/lfm2.5-vl-450m-adapters/norm --key norm [--shift]
```

(Call the env's `python` directly: `mamba run` takes `--n` for itself.) [Reproducing our results](reproduce.md#fine-tuning-the-vision-models)
has the rest.

## LFM3-VL-2B, in a sandbox

Same scenes, same recipe, thinking off, and this time the model repo's own Python had to run
(`trust_remote_code`), pinned at revision `ba44bd5`, every file checked by sha256
(`scripts/download-hf-weights.sh lfm3vl`).
[`sandbox.sh`](../bench/finetune/sandbox.sh) runs it in Docker with the GPU, no network, a read-only
root and code, not as root, one writable folder. A search of the 3,328 lines of Python found no
network, shell or `eval` calls; they were not read line by line, so the sandbox is what we rely on.
Its Triton kernels need a C compiler at run time, so inside the sandbox it runs on plain torch.

| What we trained | Trainable parameters | Training time | Peak VRAM | 🛢️ | 🖱️ | 🤏 | 🚦 | ⏲️ | **All** (clean) | Degraded pictures | Per decision |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| nothing (the base model, thinking off) | 0 | — | — | 8 | 28 | 29 | 22 | 10 | **97** | — | 0.8 s |
| **LoRA**, 1 seed | 15.4M (0.8%) | 25 min | 4.86 GiB | 30 | 30 | 29 | 30 | 30 | **149** | **149** | 0.6 s |

Out of 150, measured 2026-10-30. The one miss, in both columns, is the same pick scene. That beats
the untouched model with thinking on (144/150 at 2.7 s, through llama.cpp) at under a quarter of the
time per decision, and the gap thinking was buying is gone: the rules of these scenes went into the
weights. It's one seed, and training and test use the same drawing code, so this shows it learned
*our scenes*, not that it got better at seeing in general. The checks below test exactly that.

### Did it hold up? (2026-10-30)

| Check | Untouched, thinking off | Tuned |
|---|---:|---:|
| Three LoRA seeds, glance | 97 | **149 / 150 / 150** |
| Three seeds, degraded pictures | — | **149 / 150 / 150** |
| Three seeds, [scenes never trained on](../bench/finetune/scenes_new.py) (new layouts, palettes, button names) | 105 | **150 / 150 / 149** |
| [Vision probe](vision-probe.md), one seed | 68% | **78%** (91% for the untouched model with thinking on) |
| Norm-only (language model, 201K values, 0.4 MB, 20 min, 4.69 GiB, lr 2e-4), one seed | 97 clean, 105 new | **150** clean, **149** new, **150** degraded |
| IA3 (196K values, 0.8 MB, 21 min, 4.63 GiB, lr 1e-2), one seed | 97 clean, 105 new | **150** clean, **147** new, **149** degraded |
| *LFM2.5-VL-450M, adapters trained on glance + grid, on new scenes* | *14* | *LoRA 110, norm-only 98 (127 and 139 on the drawings they trained on)* |

So it isn't memorising our drawing code, and it forgot nothing. The tiny methods do the job on the
2B model as well: about 75 times fewer values than LoRA and within three scenes of it. The misses
on new scenes are all tanks: IA3's three refill both A and B. They run in llama.cpp too,
[baked into the weights](#serving-it-through-llamacpp). The 450M model tells a different story: its
adapters do far better than its 14/150 base, but they lose 17 and 41 points on drawings they never
saw, the norm-only one the most, so its near-perfect clean scores overstated how much it had
learned. Those LFM2.5 adapters shared their training with the grid, so they aren't a like-for-like
match for LFM3-VL's glance-only ones. One adapter trained on glance and
the grid together does both jobs:

| LFM3-VL-2B, trained on both (one seed) | Glance | New scenes | Grid open | Mazes | Mazes, harness skips blocked moves | Mazes in `llama-server` | Training | Peak VRAM |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| none | 97 | 105 | 3/8 (served) | — | — | 0/8 | | |
| LoRA | 150 | 148 | 8/8 | 3/8 | 5/8 | 1/8 | 47 min | 4.86 GiB |
| **Norm-only** | 150 | 148 | 8/8 | **6/8** | 6/8 | **5/8** | 44 min | 4.69 GiB |
| **IA3** | 150 | 149 | 8/8 | 5/8 | **7/8** | 4/8 | 41 min | 4.63 GiB |

The smallest adapters beat LoRA at steering and match it at the glance, the same result as on
LFM2.5 but without its unsteady glance. Before the harness rule norm-only hits a wall 32 times
over the 16 grids, against LoRA's 96. IA3 with the rule, 7/8, is the best maze score in this repo.
Steps stay close to the shortest path on what they solve (1.03 to 1.13 times it). That's one seed
each over 8 mazes, so the order of the three is a rough reading, and the LFM2.5 seeds above varied
by more than these gaps on the glance.

Baked into a GGUF and served, the glance holds (norm-only 150 and 148, IA3 150 and 149) but mazes
slip: norm-only and IA3 lose one, LoRA two (`--lora`, on stock). The server samples rather than taking
the top move, so this looks like sampling, though no run here separates that from the
format change. Open grids stay 8/8, at 0.3 s a step. The harness rule isn't in the served runs.

**In llama.cpp.** `convert_lora_to_gguf.py` from our fork makes a 31 MB adapter, and it runs on the
stock model with no sandbox: 149/150, 0.42 s a decision, 3.2 GB of VRAM. With the adapter's scale
set to 0, a 30-scene sample fell to 21/30, so it really is applied.

    QMODEL=lfm3vl EXTRA_ARGS="--lora models/lfm3-vl-2b-adapters/lora.gguf" bash scripts/run-qwen.sh --image

Ask with thinking off (`chat_template_kwargs: {"enable_thinking": false}`). The adapters aren't in
the repo; `sandbox.sh train` rebuilds one in about 25 minutes.


## Steering vs training (2026-11-01)

A [steering vector](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.06681v2) is a small nudge added to every layer's output.
It's built from pairs of contrasting prompts, with no gradient training: under a minute on the CPU, and
190 KB. We tried it because it's far cheaper than the 25 minutes an adapter takes. It works on LFM3, but the safe range is narrow, and it
didn't fix the doom loops.

**It works here.** `llama-cvector-generator` from our fork runs on both LFM3-1.7B and LFM3-VL-2B, and the
server applies the result with `--control-vector-scaled FILE:STRENGTH`. Speed didn't change: 105 tokens
a second with the vector on the GPU.

| Vector, model | Strength | Mean tokens per answer | Facts right |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Concise, LFM3-1.7B | 0 | 217 | 2/2 |
| | +0.05 | **151** | 2/2 |
| | +0.1 | answers start to break | |
| | -0.05 | 283 | 1/2 |
| | ±0.5 and beyond | blank or word salad | 0/2 |
| Try something else, LFM3-VL-2B | 0 | 122 | 2/2 |
| | +0.03 | **90** | 2/2 |
| | +0.06 | answers start to break | |
| | +0.1 | broken | |

That's 8 questions per strength, so a coarse check. One git answer at +0.05 also read weaker, and the
two fact questions wouldn't catch that. The usable strength is far below the 0.8 in
[the tool's example](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/tree/master/tools/cvector-generator), so sweep from small numbers. The second vector cut answer length as well, so these
vectors move more than the one habit they were built from.

**It didn't fix doom loops.** The "try something else" vector at +0.03 on pibench, against plain
LFM3-VL:

| | Plain | With the vector |
|---|---:|---:|
| Solved | 0/18 | **0/6** |
| Calls that copy an earlier call (median) | 93% | 80% |
| Longest streak of one call, worst run | 769 | 129 |
| Longest streak of one call, median run | 3 | 8 |
| Timeouts | 14/18 at 20 min | 4/6 at 15 min |
| Context | 64K | 32K |

The agent still spends most of its calls repeating itself. The drop in repeats is a weak signal: there
are 6 runs against 18, and a shorter time limit alone caps the worst streak, so read the 769 vs 129 with
care. It solved nothing either way, and its best task, feature-inventory, stayed at 7 of 8 tests.

**Where steering stands against training:** training taught LFM3-VL a visual task it couldn't do, and
a vector can't add a skill like that. A vector is a quick way to shift a habit, at a strength you have
to find by hand, and worth trying when that's all you need. The results are in
[`bench/steering/`](../bench/steering/), the commands in [Reproducing our results](reproduce.md#steering).

## Can LFM3-VL do more than look? (2026-11-01)

LFM3-VL reads pictures well and scores 0/18 on pibench; LFM3-1.7B scores 12/18 and has no eyes. We tried
to get one setup that does both, cheapest test first. Temperature ended the doom loops but didn't make
VL an agent, and no fine-tune did either. Pairing the two models works, and Gemma 4 26B already does
both by itself. Every test here is a pilot, so read the ranges, not the
points.

**Why VL loops.** We replayed 13 real stuck conversations, 4 samples each, and counted exact repeats of
the stuck call. The two models share 287 tensor shapes, but 286 tensors differ: mean cosine about 0.8
and relative distance about 0.6, or 0.46 and 1.0 for the token embeddings. So they're close relatives
trained apart.

| Same 13 stuck points | Repeats the call | Points where it repeats |
|---|---:|---:|
| LFM3-VL, temperature 0.1 | 32/52 | 11/13 |
| LFM3-VL, temperature 0.7 | 8/52 | 5/13 |
| LFM3-1.7B, temperature 0.1 | **0/52** | **0/13** |

So the cause is in the weights. One trap: our first replay left the thinking out of the history and
showed only 15/52. Real prompts include it, and recorded token counts prove that. So reasoning
that stays in the history feeds the loops, and any replay has to include it.

**VL on pibench.** All at 6 runs, against plain VL's 18. The plain runs had 64K of context, and the other
three 32K, which we only noticed later. No run in those three passed 28K, so that shouldn't have changed them, but it's
a second difference besides the time limit:

| Setup | Solved | Tests passed | Calls that copy an earlier one | Worst streak | Timeouts |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Plain, temperature 0.1, 20 min | 0/18 | 76/162 | 93% | 769 | 14/18 |
| [Steering vector](#steering-vs-training-2026-11-01), 15 min | 0/6 | 7/28 | 80% | 129 | 4/6 |
| **Temperature 0.7, 15 min** | 0/6 | 30/54 | **24%** | **3** | 3/6 |
| Temperature 0.7 + agent LoRA, 15 min | 0/6 | 27/54 | 48% | 34 | 3/6 |

- Temperature is the doom loop fix, and it costs nothing: `TEMP=0.7`. Scrape-books also passed
  tests for the first time (2/5, always 0/5 before). Even so, the shorter limit and smaller context mean the streak
  numbers aren't a fair match.
- It's still not an agent. 0/6 only says the true rate is likely under 46%, but it's
  clearly below LFM3-1.7B's 12/18 (Fisher p = 0.01). It now fails on quality, by stopping early or
  checking badly, instead of looping.
- The agent LoRA didn't pay off. We trained VL for 24 minutes on 177 turns from LFM3-1.7B's solved
  runs, plus examples of getting out of a loop. On held-out stuck points, repeats fell from 21/24 to
  7/24, and vision held (101/150 against 110, p = 0.31). On pibench it was no better than temperature
  alone. Loss fell from 0.84 to 0.25, so it likely memorised 177 examples rather than learning a habit.

**Giving LFM3-1.7B eyes.**

| Test | Score | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| VL's projector on LFM3-1.7B, as it is | 14/150 | Blind: answers restate the prompt |
| A new projector, 15 min on 1500 scenes | 35/150 | Still blind: one identical answer to all 30 tanks, signals and gauges |
| Overfit that projector on 40 scenes, 30 passes | loss 0.15 | Still falling slowly, not clearly learning |

The 35/150 isn't progress: 20/30 on signals is what always answering `stop` gets. The
projector alone didn't teach 1.7B to read pictures. Whether more data would is still open, but
[LLaVA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08485) reportedly needed hundreds of thousands of examples, so
it's not a cheap test. Loading 1.7B into VL's code was the easy part: mapping the GGUF back
reproduced all 286 of VL's text tensors.

**What does work.** One-picture decisions with thinking on, against pibench:

| Setup | Glance | pibench | VRAM | Time per decision |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| **Gemma 4 26B** (projector on CPU, 32K context) | **30/30** | **18/18** | 6.2 GB | 17 s |
| Groot-19B | 27/30 | 18/18 | 7.2 GB | 16 s |
| **LFM3-1.7B drives, VL reports in words** | **57/60** | 12/18 for the driver | **5.0 GB** both | 16 s |
| LFM3-VL alone | 60/60 | 0/18 | 2.6 GB | |

Gemma's and Groot's 30 scenes are the first 6 of each kind, and their ranges are wide: Gemma's 30/30
means at least 88%, and 30/30 against 27/30 isn't a real gap (p = 0.24). Gemma was also quicker than we expected,
since it took 70 s an image on the [vision probe](vision-probe.md), which is a harder
test. All three take about the same time, so the two-model pair's edge is memory, not speed. Its
driver never sees a picture, only VL's report, and all 3 of its misses were tanks. The pibench score for
the pair is LFM3-1.7B's alone: nobody has run a task that needs both yet.

Every test here can be repeated: see [Reproducing our results](reproduce.md#can-lfm3-vl-do-more-than-look).
# 🧰 Getting started

From "fresh Linux box" to "a 35B model writing code on an 8 GB laptop GPU" in about an hour,
most of which is your internet connection and a compiler doing their thing while you make
coffee.

## What you need

| | Minimum | What we used |
|---|---|---|
| GPU | NVIDIA, 8 GB VRAM, driver that supports CUDA ≥ 12.2 (`nvidia-smi` → "CUDA Version") | RTX 2070 Max-Q, 8 GB (Turing, `sm_75`) |
| RAM | 16 GB for the small models; **32 GB** for the big MoEs (their experts live in RAM) | 32 GB |
| Disk | ~5 GB per small model, ~15–25 GB per big one, ~4 GB per llama.cpp build | plenty, eventually not |
| OS | Linux | Ubuntu 22.04 |
| Tools | [Miniforge](https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge) (`mamba`), `git`, Node.js ≥ 22.19 for pi | |

> **Why so much RAM for an "8 GB" project?** Because the trick for the big models is to keep
> the bulky expert weights in system RAM and only the busy parts on the GPU. The 8 GB is the
> VRAM budget; the RAM is where the rest of the elephant sits. See [how it fits](how-it-fits.md).

## 1. Clone

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ricardodeazambuja/8gb-vram-ai-agent-adventures
cd 8gb-vram-ai-agent-adventures
```

## 2. The CUDA build toolchain (once)

All builds use the same pinned toolchain: 220 conda-forge packages (CUDA toolkit 12.2.2,
nvcc 12.2.140, gcc/g++ 12.4), URLs and md5 sums in `envs/llamacpp-cuda.linux-64.lock`.
Nothing system-wide is touched.

```bash
mamba create -y -n llamacpp-cuda --file envs/llamacpp-cuda.linux-64.lock
```

## 3. Build llama.cpp (once per flavour)

Every model runs on one build of our llama.cpp fork, cloned next to this repo. The
fork carries the model PRs we use on top of a pinned upstream commit, and the launch scripts find
it there, or set `ADV_LLAMA=/path`. `build-llama-cuda.sh` builds it by default at the commit
everything here was tested on. Build only what you need
(~10–20 min each; free the GPU first, the build ends with a quick smoke test).

| Build | For | Command |
|---|---|---|
| `../llama.cpp`, **our fork**: [`ricardodeazambuja/llama.cpp`](https://github.com/ricardodeazambuja/llama.cpp), branch [`adventures`](https://github.com/ricardodeazambuja/llama.cpp/tree/adventures) (upstream 2026-10-28 + LocateAnything PR #24749 + Dory PR #30306 + [PrismML's fork](https://github.com/PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp) for `PTQ1_0`) | Bonsai 2 27B, Gemma 4 26B, Qwen3.6, the Qwen3.8 distill, Qwen3-VL, Dory, Spark, Flint, Nemotron Bolt 74B and E4B, Groot, Ornith, LFM2.5-24B, LFM3, LFM3-VL, LocateAnything, Clef-Flash | `CUDA_VER=12.2 bash scripts/build-llama-cuda.sh` |

`CUDA_VER=12.2` matters: without it the script picks your driver's CUDA version for the
toolkit. The GPU architecture is auto-detected (override with `CUDA_ARCH=86` and friends).

## 4. A small env for downloads

The download scripts use the Hugging Face `hf` command from a conda env called `llamacpp`:

```bash
mamba create -y -n llamacpp -c conda-forge huggingface_hub
```

(`bash scripts/setup.sh` also creates this env, plus a Vulkan llama.cpp as a no-compiler
fallback, and downloads Gemma 4 26B. If a download script complains there's no `hf` in the
env, install a recent `huggingface_hub` into it; any env name works with `ENV_NAME=`.)

## 5. Download a model

Every download is pinned to an exact Hugging Face revision and checked against its sha256, so
you get the very bytes we tested. A file that fails the check is deleted, because a GGUF can
load fine and still be corrupt (we learned this the [hard way](troubleshooting.md)).

```bash
bash scripts/setup.sh                                  # Gemma 4 26B-A4B (~14 GB) + the llamacpp env
bash scripts/download-models.sh flint            # or any of: qwen36 distill bonsai2 qwen3vl dory spark flint bolt e4b
```


## 6. Install pi and point it at your laptop

[pi](https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono) is the coding agent that drives the models: it reads
files, runs commands and edits code, all against a local OpenAI-compatible server.

```bash
npm i -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent
bash scripts/configure-pi.sh      # registers the "llamacpp" provider in ~/.pi/agent/models.json
```

The launchers keep pi's context window in sync with the server, so you only do this once.

## 7. Go

Every model has a one-word launcher that starts the server, waits for it, and opens pi:

```bash
BACKEND=cuda bash scripts/start.sh     # Gemma 4 26B-A4B
bash scripts/start_flint.sh            # Flint
QMODEL=qwen36 bash scripts/start-qwen.sh
```

The full list is in the [README](../README.md#the-lineup). One model at a time: they all
listen on port 8080 and each one wants the whole GPU. When you're done:

```bash
bash scripts/stop-server.sh
```

Want a one-shot answer instead of a chat? Any pi flag passes through:

```bash
bash scripts/start_flint.sh -p "explain @README.md" --no-session </dev/null
```

(The `</dev/null` is not decoration: without it `pi -p` waits for input that never comes.)

## Something went sideways?

Check [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md). It's a list of every way this went wrong for us,
which turned out to be a long list.
# 🪦 The graveyard (things that didn't make it)

Every working model on [the scoreboard](scoreboard.md) is standing on a small pile of things that
didn't work. They're here so you don't have to dig the same holes. Each one gets a fair
headstone: what we tried, the numbers, and the cause of death.

All measured on the same laptop: RTX 2070 Max-Q (8 GB), 32 GB RAM. A useful number to keep in
mind first: on this rig the *same* configuration, run again, scatters by about **±13%**. Any
"speedup" smaller than that is a rumour.

---

The weights for Qwen3.8-27B, DiffusionGemma and Nemotron-Labs-Diffusion-8B were deleted on 2026-11-01, and
their launcher and download entries are gone. These notes are what remains of them.

## The Gemma 4 12B fine-tunes: tool-calling template, no agent

**Tried:** two community fine-tunes of `gemma-4-12B-it` by the same hobbyist, the
[coder (v1)](models/gemma4-12b-coder.md) and the [agentic model (v2)](models/gemma4-12b-agentic.md), dense, 40 of
48 layers on the GPU at 32K (13 tok/s), or all 48 with the TurboQuant fork's `turbo3` V cache at 16K (28 tok/s).

**Numbers:** 0/18 on [pibench](pibench.md) for both (2026-11-02). The coder made 6 tool calls in 18 jobs and never
wrote a file. The agentic model made 31 a job, ran out of the 20 minutes in 11 of 18, and sent 57 calls with no file
path.

**Cause of death:** the models, not the harness. Replaying pi's captured requests with no grammar or parser
([probe](../bench/gemma12-probe/README.md)) showed the coder narrating its steps as text instead of calling tools,
and the agentic model writing clean calls most of the time and broken ones now and then. The coder was trained on
single-function coding problems only; the agentic model's one supporting number is a 20-task benchmark its author
ran. Both retired on 2026-11-02, with their launchers, download entries and the TurboQuant build.

## Qwen3.8-27B, dense, in i-quants: 2.5 tok/s

**Tried:** Qwen's dense 27B as ISTA-DASLab's GSQ-RCO `IQ3_XXS-mtp` file (10.4 GB), split by whole
layers: 30 of 64 on the GPU, the rest on the CPU, 32K context.

**Numbers:** 2.5 tok/s. Tool calls worked; the answer arrived, eventually. Pushing to 36 GPU
layers crashed on the first request (CUDA out of memory in the cuBLAS workspace). A `llama-bench`
sweep fit a line of ~0.110 s + 0.008 s per CPU-resident layer, i.e. ~9 tok/s only if all 64
layers were on the GPU. Hyperthreads made it worse; its built-in MTP head made it worse too
(2.0 tok/s, 57% draft acceptance).

**Cause of death:** the CPU half was **compute-bound, not bandwidth-bound**: 600% CPU (all 6
cores), GPU at 20%, only ~14 GB/s of RAM traffic. The file is mostly IQ1/IQ2/IQ3 i-quants, which
are expensive to decode on a CPU. The pre-test estimate (6–8 tok/s from bandwidth) was wrong by
~3× for exactly that reason.

**Afterlife:** the same model lives on as [Bonsai 2 27B](models/bonsai2-27b.md): ternary weights,
5.95 GB, all on the GPU, ~27 tok/s.

## ik_llama.cpp for that same 27B: slower

**Tried:** the ik_llama.cpp fork, whose README promises better CPU performance and faster
i-quant kernels, built with the same toolchain.

**Numbers:** 1.75 tok/s vs mainline's 2.63 at 30 GPU layers; CPU-only 0.93 vs 1.01.

**Cause of death:** its CPU i-quant kernels were not faster on this file. One fork, zero wins.

## Gemma 4 MTP self-speculation: real, but not at temperature 1.0

**Tried:** Gemma 4's Multi-Token Prediction head (a 0.25 GB draft that ships inside Unsloth's
QAT repo) with `--spec-type draft-mtp`. Speculative decoding is lossless by construction, so the
only question is speed.

**Numbers:** at greedy (temp 0), 79–88% draft acceptance and **+15–31%** decode (≈21 → 27
tok/s). At the default temp 1.0, acceptance falls to 66–74% and the gain is **within noise**. And
`--spec-draft-p-min > 0` is a trap: with sampling it collapsed the output into `fmt-fmt-fmt…`
loops, which look like 85–100% acceptance because degenerate text is very easy to predict.

**Cause of death:** not dead, just unemployed. It's a win for greedy or low-temperature coding,
and buys nothing at the sampling settings these models are meant to run with. If you want it:
`--spec-type draft-mtp --spec-draft-n-max 2 --spec-draft-p-min 0`.

## EAGLE3 for Gemma 4: slower, unstable, crashes

**Tried:** the `RedHatAI/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-speculator.eagle3` draft, converted to GGUF, on a
llama.cpp build (`88a3927`) with fresh EAGLE3 support.

**Numbers:** ~42% acceptance (vs MTP's ~80%, and not a quantization artefact). Its draft is a
full transformer layer, so it was **slower than no speculation even at greedy** (−19% at n-max 3,
−34% at n-max 5).

**Cause of death:** temp 1.0 degenerated, and the chat-template path (which is how pi talks to the
server) segfaulted. Bonus trap: one "fix confirmed" test was really an empty reply hiding the
crash. Always check that it *generates*, not just that it stays alive.

## DiffusionGemma 26B-A4B: 5.2 tok/s

**Tried:** a block-diffusion model on the Gemma 4 base, run through an open llama.cpp PR's
`llama-diffusion-cli` plus a small shim so pi could talk to it. It worked end to end.

**Numbers:** best config (`NCMOE=27`, flash attention on auto) ≈ **5.2 tok/s**, against ~30 tok/s
for normal decoding of the same split: 5.8× slower. 26 GPU expert layers ran out of memory; forcing
flash attention on made it 2.7× slower. pi's default system prompt didn't even fit in its 4K
context.

**Cause of death:** diffusion spends roughly **66×** the per-token compute of autoregressive
decoding (a 256-token canvas, ~21 denoising steps, 2 blocks for a 162-token answer). With most
experts on the CPU, that multiplier lands on the slowest hardware in the box. Lesson that shaped
everything after: **diffusion only pays when the whole model fits in VRAM.**

## Nemotron-Labs-Diffusion-8B: fast on code, broken tool calls

**Tried:** NVIDIA's tri-mode 8B (autoregressive, block diffusion, or self-speculation where a
diffusion pass drafts and an AR pass verifies), Q4_K_M at 5.2 GB, fully on the GPU, on the
spiritbuun/buun-llama-cpp fork. It fit, as the previous headstone demanded.

**Numbers:** plain AR decoding 41.8 tok/s. Self-speculation with 16-token drafts: **60.7 tok/s on
code (1.45×)**, but at best 27.7 tok/s on prose (0.66×) because most drafts get rejected. Block
diffusion ~16.5 tok/s (0.4×) on everything. The server path hardcodes 4-token drafts: 42.3 on
code, 28.0 on prose.

**Cause of death:** tool calls don't parse. The model writes `<function=…>` without the
`<tool_call>` wrapper its own template expects, and keeps generating past the call. An agent
model that can't call tools is just a very fast essay writer. Not adopted.

## FlashMap drafter: unfinished

**Tried:** porting FlashMap, a ~500M "flow-map" speculative drafter (from a paper reporting
4–6× speedups on an H200), to llama.cpp for the first time, on Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M. It ran end to end
in 7.65 GB of VRAM.

**Numbers:** mean acceptance length ~3.2 tokens on math (paper: 7.2–7.7) and ~1.5 on prose
(paper: 3.1–3.3). Net: math +50% (59–63 vs 39–42 tok/s), code ±0, prose −25–35%; overall
39.0 vs 39.8 tok/s for the target alone.

**Cause of death:** not dead, in a coma. Target quantization, the adaptive draft-depth controller
and the fused single-graph cycle were all ruled out; the prime suspect is a numerical bug in the
drafter-graph port. The next step is one instrumented NumPy-vs-ggml comparison of a single draft
step, not more benchmark sweeps.

## Gemma 4 12B coder at Q2_K: fits, runs, talks nonsense

**Tried:** the smallest quant of the 12B coder fine-tune. It fits entirely on the GPU and runs at
~19 tok/s.

**Numbers:** degenerate output on every path: raw prompts, the chat template at temp 0.7, the
native turn format. Things like `l. l. l.`, CJK noise, or rambling to the token limit.

**Cause of death:** the quant is simply too aggressive for this model. Q4_K_M of the same model
writes clean, correct code. Skip Q2_K; the run script refuses it anyway.

## Vulkan as the default backend: 5× slower

**Tried:** the zero-build path, conda's Vulkan llama.cpp, which works on any driver.

**Numbers:** ~4.9 tok/s on Gemma 4 26B vs **~23.5 tok/s** with a source-built CUDA backend at the
identical CPU/GPU split. Pure CPU is ~1.9.

**Cause of death:** not dead, demoted. Vulkan is still the fallback when you can't build CUDA, but
on this Turing laptop its MoE path is the bottleneck. Build CUDA (see
[getting started](getting-started.md)).

## Written up elsewhere

These failed too, and the full story is on their own pages:

- **Giving LFM3-1.7B eyes:** grafting LFM3-VL's vision on left it blind, and so did training a new
  projector.
- **Making LFM3-VL an agent:** a LoRA from solved runs solved 0/6, and `TEMP=0.7` ended the loops but
  still solved 0/6.

Both are in [Can LFM3-VL do more than look?](fine-tuning.md#can-lfm3-vl-do-more-than-look-2026-11-01)

## The ones we never let in the door

Checked and skipped on paper, so you don't have to:

- **Qwen3.8-Flash-Next** (REAP-288 / GSQ-RCO builds): the smallest files are 58–66 GB+, more than
  32 GB RAM + 8 GB VRAM.
- **Qwen3.8-Whittle-16B:** its own card calls it a "non-starter for agent and long multi-turn use:
  repetition looping".
- **Whittle-Qwen-3.8-35B-A3B:** a one-person research preview trained on 4K-token rows whose own
  table doesn't beat stock Qwen3.6-35B-A3B.
- **LLaDA2.2-mini:** a 16B MoE diffusion model, ~9 GB at Q4: experts on the CPU, i.e. the
  DiffusionGemma problem again.
- **Gemma 4 31B (dense):** no experts to park in RAM, so most of it would run on the CPU; the
  estimate was ~1–3 tok/s (not measured). For this card the realistic upgrade is a
  higher-precision quant of the 26B MoE, not a bigger model.

## Moved back in 🏠

**VibeThinker 1.5B / 3B** had their own repo for a while,
[vibethinker_tests](https://github.com/ricardodeazambuja/vibethinker_tests), and are now a
[model page](models/vibethinker.md) here again.
# 🧳 How do you fit a 40B model in 8 GB?

Short answer: you don't. Not all at once, anyway. You fit *the part that matters right now* and
let the rest live somewhere cheaper. This page is the packing list: every trick that got a model
on [the scoreboard](scoreboard.md) running on one RTX 2070 Max-Q (8 GB VRAM) with 32 GB of system
RAM.

Two things fight over those 8 GB, and you will meet them on every page of this repo:

```
 8 GB of VRAM
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ model weights (the brain)  │ KV cache (the memory of the   │ compute │
 │                            │ conversation, grows with ctx) │ buffers │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
       ↑ shrink with quantization,     ↑ shrink with smarter attention
         or move to RAM                  and KV quantization
```

Everything below is a way to make one of those boxes smaller, or to move it out of the GPU
without making everything slow.

---

## 1. The big one: park the experts in RAM

Most of the big models here are **Mixture-of-Experts** (MoE). Instead of one giant feed-forward
block per layer they have a crowd of small "experts", and a router picks a handful per token.
Gemma 4 26B-A4B, for example, routes each token to **8 of 128** experts per layer. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
and Flint have 256 experts per layer and pick 8 and 6 of them, plus one shared expert.
Nemotron Bolt 74B picks 9 of 240–432 per layer, which is how 74B parameters become ~9B per token.

So the experts are most of the *parameters* but only a sliver of them is *touched* per token.
llama.cpp exploits that by putting different tensors on different devices:

| What | Where | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Attention, embeddings, router, norms | GPU | small, used by every token |
| KV cache (your context) | GPU | grows with context, needs fast access |
| **Expert weights** | **system RAM** | the bulk of the model, but sparse per token |

The flags:

- `--cpu-moe`: **all** layers' experts go to RAM. Least VRAM, slowest.
- `--n-cpu-moe N`: the experts of the **first N** layers go to RAM, the rest stay on the GPU.
  Lower N = more experts on the GPU = faster, but more VRAM.

In this repo that's the `NCMOE` knob. Leave it empty for "all experts in RAM":

```bash
QMODEL=qwen36 NCMOE= bash scripts/run-qwen.sh   # every expert in RAM: least VRAM
QMODEL=qwen36 bash scripts/run-qwen.sh          # default NCMOE=32: 8 layers' experts on the GPU
```

What it trades, measured on Flint (128K context, q8_0 KV):

| Experts on the GPU | VRAM | Generation |
|---|---:|---:|
| none (`--cpu-moe`) | 4.4 GB | 22.2 tok/s |
| 7 layers (`NCMOE=42`) | 6.6 GB | 24.9 tok/s |
| 9 layers (`NCMOE=40`) | 7.2 GB | 26.1 tok/s |

Notice the gain is modest: the CPU still does most of the expert work. Each layer's experts cost
Flint ~300 MiB of VRAM. The per-model defaults (`NCMOE=32` for Qwen3.6, `34` for the distill,
`42` for Flint) are the measured "fits with ~0.5 GB to spare next to a desktop" values.

**Why isn't RAM bandwidth the bottleneck?** Because only a few experts fire per token. On
Gemma 4 26B at 25 tok/s with 22 of 30 layers' experts in RAM, that works out to roughly 15 GB/s of
RAM reads, comfortably under dual-channel DDR4. Measure before you believe "it must be
memory-bound": an earlier guess here was wrong by exactly that.

## 2. Quantization, in plain words

A model's weights are numbers. At full precision each one takes 16 bits. Quantization stores them
with fewer bits and a little shared scale per small group, trading a bit of accuracy for a lot of
size.

- **Q8_0 / Q6_K / Q5_K / Q4_K_M:** 8, ~6, ~5 and ~4.5 bits per weight. Q4_K_M is the usual "sweet
  spot" (the Gemma 12B fine-tunes, Qwen3-VL-8B). Q8_0 is close to lossless (Spark, 4.4 GB).
- **Unsloth "UD" quants (UD-Q4_K_XL, UD-Q3_K_XL, UD-IQ4_XS):** mixed precision, spending more bits
  on the tensors that matter. Flint runs as UD-Q3_K_XL (18.5 GB), because UD-Q4_K_XL (25 GB) would
  leave the page cache no room in 32 GB of RAM.
- **QAT (quantization-aware training):** the model was trained knowing it would be quantized, so
  the 4-bit file keeps much more quality. That's why Gemma 4 26B-A4B is used as a QAT file.
- **i-quants (IQ1–IQ4):** squeeze harder with lookup tables. Great on a GPU, **expensive to decode
  on a CPU**. That killed the dense Qwen3.8-27B here (see [the graveyard](graveyard.md)).
- **Ternary (PTQ1_0):** every weight is −1, 0 or +1, with one FP16 scale per 128 weights, about
  1.72 bits per weight. Bonsai 2 squeezes the whole Qwen3.8-27B into **5.95 GB** this way, small
  enough for the entire model to sit on the GPU. The type comes from PrismML's llama.cpp fork,
  which we merged into ours.

Rule of thumb from our scars: **the most aggressive quant of a model can simply break it.** The
Gemma 12B coder's Q2_K fits on the GPU and runs at ~19 tok/s, and produces nonsense.

## 3. The KV cache, and why some models get long context for free

The KV cache is the model's memory of the conversation: for every token in the context, every
attention layer keeps a key and a value. So its size is roughly:

```
KV bytes per token = (layers with full attention) × 2 (K and V) × KV heads × head dim × bytes per value
```

The trick is in the first factor. Many new models only give *some* layers a long memory:

- **Sliding-window attention (SWA):** most layers only look at the last few hundred or thousand
  tokens, so their cache stops growing. Gemma 4 (1024-token windows), Spark (3 of every 4 layers
  use a 512-token window), Flint (35 of 49 layers, 513-token window), Dory (1024-token windows).
- **Linear attention / Gated DeltaNet:** the Qwen3.6/3.8 family runs three of every four layers
  with a fixed-size recurrent state and **no KV cache at all**.
- **Mamba layers:** Nemotron Bolt 74B goes furthest. Of its 88 layers, 40 are Mamba-2 (a fixed
  ~160 MB of state, whatever the context), 40 are experts, and only 8 are attention, with 2 KV
  heads each.
- **Shared KV:** the Bolt E4B (a Gemma 4 E4B underneath) uses 512-token windows in most layers,
  and its last 18 layers reuse the caches of earlier ones.

Here is what that buys, per token of context (computed from each model's config):

| Model | Layers with a long KV cache | f16 | q8_0 | 128K at q8_0 |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Nemotron Bolt 74B | **8 of 88** | 8 KiB | ~4.25 KiB | **0.57 GB** |
| Nemotron Bolt E4B | 4 of 42 | 16 KiB | ~8.5 KiB | 1.14 GB (it runs 64K: 0.57 GB) |
| Qwen3.6-35B-A3B / distill | 10 of 40 | 20 KiB | ~10.6 KiB | 1.42 GB |
| Flint | 14 of 49 | 28 KiB | ~15 KiB | ~1.9 GB |
| Spark-X2.6-4B | 9 of 36 | — | ~19 KiB | see §5 |
| Qwen3.8-27B / Bonsai 2 | 16 of 64 | 64 KiB | 34 KiB | 4.55 GB |
| Qwen3-VL-8B (no tricks) | **36 of 36** | 144 KiB | ~77 KiB | doesn't fit |

Same 8 GB, wildly different context: the 74B Bolt gets 128K for about half a gigabyte and the
Qwen3.6 MoE for 1.42 GB, while Qwen3-VL-8B,
with plain attention in every layer, tops out at 16K (2.42 GB at f16, 1.28 GB at q8_0).

Side effect worth knowing: on Gemma 4 26B, **64K decodes at essentially the same speed as 32K**
(~22–25 tok/s either way). Decode speed is set by where the experts live, not by the context
length.

## 4. Quantize the KV cache too (usually)

`KVQUANT=q8_0` stores the cache at ~8.5 bits instead of 16, roughly halving it. It's a
long-context lever: at 32K Gemma's cache is ~0.6 GB and quantizing it is noise, but at 128K it
frees about 2 GB, enough to move more experts back to the GPU:

| Gemma 4 26B context | f16 KV | q8_0 KV | Gain |
|---|---|---|---|
| 65536 | `NCMOE=22` → 26.1 tok/s | `NCMOE=20` → 30.5 tok/s | +17% |
| 131072 | `NCMOE=27` (only fit) → 19.5 tok/s | `NCMOE=22` → 27.0 tok/s | +38% |

Two catches:

- **It needs flash attention.** llama.cpp won't quantize the V cache without it, so any `KVQUANT`
  turns `-fa` on.
- **It's not always allowed.** PrismML's own build (Bonsai 2) had no Turing (sm_75) code for its
  q8_0 flash-attention kernel: the server loaded fine, then aborted on the first request with *"no
  device code compatible with CUDA arch 750"*, so Bonsai ran an **f16** cache and stopped at 16K.
  With PrismML's code merged into our fork, q8_0 works, and Bonsai gets 32K in 7.1 GB.

## 5. The hidden tax: flash attention's f16 copy

Spark-X2.6-4B looked like a 128K model on paper: Q8_0 weights plus ~19 KiB/token of q8_0 KV. In
practice 128K ran out of memory by about 165 MiB. The culprit: with a *quantized* KV cache, flash
attention works on an **f16 copy of one layer's K and V**, which lives in the compute buffer and
grows with context (~5 KiB per token here). The compute buffer went from 218 MiB at 32K to
697 MiB at 128K.

So a q8_0 cache really cost Spark ~24 KiB/token, not 19. Solved for ~0.3 GB of headroom, that
gives Spark's default of **110592 tokens (108K)**. Lesson: the KV size you calculate is a floor,
not a budget.

## 6. Big batches for big prompts (on RAM-expert MoEs)

Generation is one token at a time; processing your prompt (prefill) is many tokens at once. With
the experts in RAM, a **big batch** lets llama.cpp ship the expert matrix multiplies to the GPU
for the whole batch. On Flint, raising the batch from 512 to 2048:

| Batch / ubatch | Experts on GPU | VRAM | Prefill (14.4K tokens) | Generation |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 512 | 9 layers (`NCMOE=40`) | 7.2 GB | 192 tok/s | 26.1 tok/s |
| **2048** | **7 layers (`NCMOE=42`)** | **7.25 GB** | **542 tok/s** | 24.3 tok/s |

Prefill nearly triples for about 1 tok/s of generation. The bigger compute buffer costs one layer
of experts, which is why Flint's default is `NCMOE=42` with batch 2048. For an agent like pi, whose
every turn re-reads a big system prompt and file dumps, that's the difference between waiting a
minute and waiting 25 seconds.

Nemotron Bolt 74B shows the same effect with every expert in RAM: 136 → 310 tok/s of prefill from
batch 2048 alone, generation unchanged at 14 tok/s. Putting expert layers on the GPU barely helps
it (13–15 tok/s whatever we tried), because per token it touches ~6.8B always-on parameters (on
the GPU) and only ~1.7B routed-expert ones (in RAM): the GPU side sets the pace.

## 7. Dense models: offload whole layers instead

A dense model has no experts to park; every token goes through every layer. The lever is
`-ngl N`: put the first N layers on the GPU and run the rest on the CPU. The Gemma 4 12B coder
(Q4_K_M, 48 layers) runs best with `NGL=42` (6 layers in RAM) at 32K context: ~17 tok/s.

| 12B coder quant | Best `-ngl` | tok/s |
|---|---|---:|
| Q4_K_M | 42 | ~17 |
| Q6_K | 32 | ~7 |
| Q8_0 | 24 | ~4.5 |

Every layer you push to the CPU is paid for on every token, so this gets slow fast. And don't
borrow the MoE flags: `--cpu-moe` does nothing on a dense model.

## 8. Or just be small: everything on the GPU

The happiest models here don't split at all:

| Model | File | Where | Speed |
|---|---|---|---:|
| Spark-X2.6-4B | Q8_0, 4.4 GB | all on GPU, 108K | ~50 tok/s |
| Qwen3-VL-8B | Q4_K_M 5.03 GB + 0.75 GB projector | all on GPU, 16K | ~38 tok/s |
| Dory1.0-4B | Q5_K_M, 3.67 GB | all on GPU, 64K | 29 tok/s (2 loops) |
| Bonsai 2 27B | ternary, 5.95 GB | all on GPU, 32K | ~38 tok/s |

No RAM traffic, no CPU math: fast, as long as you can live with the context.

### Dory's surprise: `-ngl 999`

Dory is a *looped* model: a block of 20 layers runs 1–4 times per token. The llama.cpp build for
it counts every loop as extra layers, so the usual "all layers" value `-ngl 99` silently leaves
weights on the CPU. At 4 loops that kept 2.9 GB of weights and most of the KV cache on the host:
3.8 tok/s instead of 19. The clue was VRAM going *down* as loops went up. `-ngl 999` fixes it, and
the repo's Dory default already uses it.

## 9. TurboQuant: an even smaller V cache

The Gemma 4 12B agentic fine-tune is dense too, and at Q4_K_M it *almost* fits. The fix was
`turbo3`, a ~3.5-bit V-cache codec (Walsh-Hadamard-rotated polar quantization) from the
[TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant](https://github.com/TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant) fork. It's a runtime
option, not a model format. With `-ctk q8_0 -ctv turbo3` (keys are touchy, values tolerate
aggression), **all 48 layers fit on the GPU**: 7.76 of 8 GB at 16K, ~28.3 tok/s, about 1.7× the
partial-offload speed.

It needed `--parallel 1 -b 512`; the default `n_parallel=4` + `n_batch=2048` OOMs. Both 12B fine-tunes were
[retired](models/gemma4-12b-agentic.md) on 2026-11-02 (0/18 on pibench), and their launchers and the TurboQuant
build with them; the numbers stay here because the trick works on any Gemma 4 GGUF.

## 10. Pick the right backend: CUDA, not Vulkan

Same model (Gemma 4 26B), same split (`NCMOE=22`), same laptop:

| Backend | Generation |
|---|---:|
| CPU only | ~1.9 tok/s |
| Vulkan (stock build) | ~4.9 tok/s |
| **CUDA (built from source)** | **~23.5 tok/s** |

CUDA is about 5× Vulkan here, far outside the usual "CUDA is a bit faster" folklore. The prebuilt
conda CUDA binaries need a newer driver than this laptop's, so `scripts/build-llama-cuda.sh` builds
llama.cpp against the driver's own CUDA version in an isolated environment. Details in
[getting started](getting-started.md).

## 11. VRAM is not the only wall 🧱

The GPU isn't the only thing that can run out. The MoE models keep 15–20 GB of experts in system
RAM, and Linux will happily let you load all of that and then **kill the server** a few seconds
later when something else needs memory. It looks like this: a clean load, pi connects, and the
server log just says `Killed`. That's the kernel's OOM killer, not the GPU.

```bash
journalctl -k | grep -i oom      # confirm it was host RAM
```

Fixes: a smaller context, a smaller quant (that's why Flint uses UD-Q3_K_XL), closing the browser
with 40 tabs, or more swap. Re-tuning `NCMOE` won't help; the GPU was never the problem. More
symptoms like this live in [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).

## 12. Let a cache pick the experts (opt-in, our fork's `moe-offload` branch)

`--n-cpu-moe` (§1) puts *fixed* layers' experts on the GPU, whether or not the next token uses them. The
GPU expert cache picks experts by use instead. Every expert lives in RAM (`--cpu-moe`), and llama.cpp keeps
the recently used ones in a VRAM cache of a size you choose (`--moe-cache-mib`; upstream PRs
[#29887](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/29887) and #30637, so already in our `adventures` build).
Consecutive tokens reuse many of the same experts: with 20% of Qwen3.6's experts in the cache, 77%
of lookups hit.

On a miss, the merged code copies the expert to the GPU. Here that is the slow part: this laptop's PCIe
3.0 link runs at x8, measured at 6.45 GB/s, and copying one expert takes ~0.23 ms. The CPU can compute the
same expert from RAM in ~0.1 ms. The open upstream PR [#30966](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/30966)
(Aman Gupta) adds `--moe-cache-cpu-share F`, which hands that share of the misses to the CPU. Copying
every miss, the cache gained ~23% on Qwen3.6 (30.2 vs 24.6 tok/s). With 95% of misses on the CPU:

| Model (context) | Now: `--n-cpu-moe` | Cache + CPU share 0.95, same VRAM | Gain |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Qwen3.6-35B-A3B (128K) | 24.9 tok/s (`NCMOE=32`) | **39.4 tok/s** (2900 MiB) | +58% |
| Gemma 4 26B-A4B (32K) | 21.0 tok/s (`NCMOE=22`) | **30.0 tok/s** (3267 MiB) | +43% |
| Flint (128K) | 26.9 tok/s (`NCMOE=42`) | **32.1 tok/s** (2168 MiB) | +19% |

Same flags otherwise (`-np 1`, flash attention, q8_0 KV; batch 512, Flint 2048); baseline, cache, baseline
again, two 400-token runs each; the table compares the first baseline. Prompt speed didn't change. VRAM
in use was 86–186 MiB lower for Qwen3.6 and within 10 MiB for the other two. The cache also held up over a 2,500-token answer: 33–37
tok/s, while `NCMOE=32` sank to 18–21 tok/s as the CPU package hit 100 °C (83 °C with the cache). Flint's
gain is the least sure: its second baseline came in at 24.2 and 17.4 tok/s.

The cache sizes match the expert bytes `--n-cpu-moe` puts on the GPU (Qwen3.6's 2900 is a little under its
3070); they are not tuned yet. Gemma at pibench's 64K is the exception: the matching 4084 MiB ran out of memory at load (and so did
`NCMOE=20` itself that day, with the desktop using ~0.7 GB), so it gets 3500, peaking at 7.5 GB after a 34K-token
prompt. The method,
scripts and raw logs are in [`bench/moe-cache/`](../bench/moe-cache/README.md).

```bash
QMODEL=qwen36 MOE_CACHE=auto bash scripts/run-qwen.sh   # the measured size per model; or a MiB number
BACKEND=cuda KVQUANT=q8_0 MOE_CACHE=3267 bash scripts/run-server.sh   # Gemma 4 26B at 32K
```

The flag isn't in upstream llama.cpp yet. Build our fork's
[`moe-offload`](https://github.com/ricardodeazambuja/llama.cpp/tree/moe-offload) branch (`adventures` +
PR #30966's 8 commits): `LLAMA_REF=3a72c57bef4ca02bfa394961f1789393c5e4e9c6 bash scripts/build-llama-cuda.sh`.
Without it, `MOE_CACHE` fails at startup with an unknown flag.

**In real agent sessions too.** A full [pibench](pibench.md) batch with `MOE_CACHE=auto` (2026-11-04,
18 attempts per model, same 64K/128K contexts as the scored batch) against the scored batch from 2026-10-23.
Decode and prefill come from pi's own record of every model call (tokens and duration), fitted as
`time = input/prefill + output/decode + overhead`:

| Model | Solved (before → cache) | Decode tok/s | Prefill tok/s | Model time, all 18 runs | Median run |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Qwen3.6 | 16/18 → 17/18 | 20.7 → **32.9** (+59%) | 213 → 204 | 66.6 → 55.3 min, with 25% more output | 3.4 → 2.6 min |
| Gemma 4 26B (3500 MiB) | 18/18 → 17/18 | 25.6 → **32.3** (+26%) | 327 → 322 | 92.7 → 75.3 min | 4.8 → 4.5 min |
| Flint | 18/18 → 18/18 | 21.5 → **26.4** (+23%) | 426 → 354 (−17%) | 87.7 → 79.0 min | 4.3 → 3.6 min |

Scores held: Gemma's one miss passed 5 of 6 hidden tests, and Qwen3.6's one fewer failure is within its
run-to-run spread. The two batches ran on different builds two weeks apart, but the decode gains agree with
the same-build A/B above. The cold start after a long prompt (the fit check in
[`bench/moe-cache/`](../bench/moe-cache/README.md)) doesn't show here: pi's turns add a few hundred
tokens to a cached prompt, which the cache handles well. Flint's prefill is the cost: batches over 32 tokens
bypass the cache and copy the used experts of all 47 MoE layers, where `NCMOE=42` keeps 7 of them on the
GPU. Shrinking that would take a different change (prefill on the CPU for some layers, or a bigger batch so
each copy serves more tokens), not a warmer cache. Numbers: [`pibench-speed.txt`](../bench/moe-cache/logs/pibench-speed.txt),
[`stats-moecache.txt`](../bench/results/stats-moecache.txt).

The idea comes from [FreeToken](https://github.com/FlashML-org/FreeToken) (read, not run: it executes
model-repo code), which pairs an expert cache with CPU-computed misses on a single GPU. Both turned out to
be in llama.cpp already.
# 🖼️ Making pictures on 8 GB

Text models aren't the only thing this laptop can run. A current image model draws a 1024×1024
picture in 23 seconds in 3.5 GB of VRAM, and it can spell. Stable Diffusion was what we'd have
reached for a few years ago; it's now the slowest of the three models we tried.

![The same four prompts on all three models](image-generation-samples.jpg)

*Left to right: FLUX.2 Klein 4B, Iris-3B Turbo, SDXL. Seed 1, shrunk to fit; nothing retouched.*

## The results

Four prompts × two seeds at 1024×1024, measured 2026-10-31:

| Model | Time per image | Peak VRAM | Steps | Hands | Sign text | Watercolour asked for |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|---|
| **FLUX.2 Klein 4B** Q4_0 | **23 s** | **3.5 GB** | 4 | good | right on both, one doubled | yes |
| **Iris-3B Turbo** (6 GB file) | 33 s | 7.3 GB | 4 | good | right on both, cleanest | no, looks like digital cartoon |
| SDXL base | 47 s | 5.8 GB | 25 | passable, messy laces | garbled on one, spacing off on the other | yes |

Times are medians; the first run of each model was 8 to 15 s slower while files loaded. The
quality columns are our own look at the pictures, not a score.

**Pick Klein.** It's the fastest, uses half the VRAM and leaves room for a LoRA or another model.
**Iris-3B Turbo is the one for text**, but at 7.3 of 7.8 GB it leaves nothing spare. Its Q4 GGUF
should use less, which we haven't tried.

The text encoder runs on the CPU (`--backend te=cpu`), since it's needed once per prompt, and VAE
tiling is on. Iris-3B paints pixels directly, so it has no VAE at all.

## Giving it a picture as well

Prompts aren't the only input. One test of each on our own 1024×1024 fisherman, 2026-10-31:

| Input | Flag | FLUX.2 Klein 4B | Iris-3B Turbo | SDXL |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Picture + prompt, redraw it | `-i in.png --strength 0.7` | works | works | works |
| Picture + mask + prompt, redraw one area | `-i in.png --mask mask.png` | **works, blends best** | pasted a hard square | works, slight seam |
| Reference + prompt, edit by instruction | `-r in.png` | **works** | ignored | ignored |

**Klein's reference edit is the one to use:** "wearing a red cap instead of his hat" changed the hat
and kept the man, the net and the sunset, though the face shifted a little. Iris-3B and SDXL run
with `-r` and report no error, but their picture is identical to a run without it, so an exit code
of 0 proves nothing there.

White in the mask is the area to redraw. Everything outside should stay put, and the average change
per colour value (out of 255) well outside the mask was 0.0 on Iris-3B, 2.5 on Klein and 5.9 on SDXL
([`run_inputs.py`](../bench/imagegen/run_inputs.py), [results](../bench/imagegen/results/inputs.json));
the two latent models go through a VAE, which we think explains that. At strength 0.7 the
"watercolour" request came out as a painted-looking photo, not a real restyle; lower strengths
keep more, and we only tried this one. ControlNet, IP-Adapter and PhotoMaker also have flags and
are untested.

## Run it yourself

The runner is [stable-diffusion.cpp](https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp), the llama.cpp
of image models. We used commit `0a067120`, cloned next to this repo:

```bash
git clone --recursive https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp ../stable-diffusion.cpp
git -C ../stable-diffusion.cpp checkout 0a067120b388bfefb9a87b96960cb548b97e9157
git -C ../stable-diffusion.cpp submodule update --init --recursive
mamba run -n llamacpp-cuda bash -c 'cd ../stable-diffusion.cpp && \
  cmake -B build -DSD_CUDA=ON -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=75 -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && \
  cmake --build build -j6'

bash scripts/download-models.sh klein kleinte kleinvae        # ~5.3 GB
python3 bench/imagegen/run_gen.py klein --quick               # one picture
python3 bench/imagegen/run_gen.py klein iris sdxl             # the table above
```

Pictures land in `models/image-gen/out/`, timings in [`bench/imagegen/results/`](../bench/imagegen/results/).
All the downloads are pinned and sha256-checked, and their audits are in
[Security](security.md#audits-so-far).

## Teaching SDXL a style or a person

An 8 KB learned word is the best first try, for a person and for a style. It's
strong and doesn't leak into prompts without the word, and it stacks. For famous styles, naming them
in the prompt is still free and at least as good, and 1000 steps of LoRA or norm-only fell short of
that. The first results below are by eye from two seeds (1 and 2), through diffusers rather than
`sd-cli`, so treat them as a first look, not a score. Later results are in
[The best person file is 8 KB](#the-best-person-file-is-8-kb) and
[Words on styles](#words-on-styles-and-stacking).

![Each row compares no adapter, the style named in the prompt, LoRA and norm-only; the last row is the person](image-style-samples.jpg)

*Seed 1. The last row's second picture is the training reference, not a generated result. "No
adapter" uses the same prompt as the adapters, trigger word included.*

| Teaching | Pictures | Named in the prompt | LoRA | Norm-only |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Van Gogh | 17 | Clear on both seeds | Paint and palette, without the swirling strokes | Slight, some paint texture |
| Juan Gris (cubism) | 23 | Clear on both seeds | Muted paint, no cubist shapes | Little, some odd geometry |
| Atget (old photo) | 24 | Clear on both seeds | Faded tones, sepia only sometimes | Little |
| A person | 20 | Can't: an invented man has no name | Face, hair, beard and glasses | Beard and glasses, often the face; weaker at full length |

- The person worked best. With no adapter, the same prompts give a stranger. Norm-only gets
  the round glasses and red beard on both seeds in the watercolour and chef pictures, from a file
  about 19x smaller. Neither method shows much through the astronaut's visor.
- It went the opposite way from our guess. We expected style to suit norm-only, because
  style-transfer research ties style to feature means and spreads. Here, it did better on the person.
- It leaks. Asked for just "a man on a beach", both person adapters drew a man in a blue shirt
  like the training pictures. Style adapters also change pictures without their trigger word,
  mostly darkening or muting them. Stacking a person with a style needs a test for this.
- The fix for the person turned out to be a word. See [below](#the-best-person-file-is-8-kb).
- Why Gris rather than Picasso: Picasso's work is still under copyright. We train only on public
  domain art, and the Atget photos are public domain too. The "person" is invented, drawn by Klein
  ([`make_subject.py`](../bench/imagegen/make_subject.py)).

| Method | Trainable | File | 1000 steps at 768 px | Peak VRAM |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| LoRA, rank 8 | 11.6M | 47 MB | 17 min | 5.4 GiB |
| Norm-only | 0.59M | 2.5 MB | 15.5 min | 5.3 GiB |

Both train in the 8 GB card by caching text and image encodings first. Numbers are in
[`style-runs.json`](../bench/imagegen/results/style-runs.json).

The `vlft` environment is [here](reproduce.md#setup).

```bash
python3 bench/imagegen/fetch_commons.py        # our public domain sets, listed in bench/imagegen/data/
mamba run -n vlft python bench/imagegen/train_style.py train --set vangogh --method norm --trigger vgstyl
mamba run -n vlft python bench/imagegen/train_style.py eval --run vangogh-norm --trigger vgstyl
```

### The best person file is 8 KB

Same invented man, 20 pictures, two seeds, judged by eye. Pictures for the new rows come from
`sd-cli` where marked, so they're what you'd actually run. There's no automatic likeness score yet.

| Method | File | Training | Likeness | Leaks into "a man on a beach"? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Klein `-r`, no training** | one photo | none | Good, weaker at full length; both seeds look alike | n/a, only with the photo |
| **Textual Inversion, `sd-cli`** | **8 KB** | 35 min | **Strong, about level with LoRA** | **No** |
| LoRA with regularisation pictures | 47 MB | 17 min, plus making 20 pictures | Strong; one astronaut lost the face | **No** |
| LoRA, `sd-cli` | 47 MB | 17 min | Strong | Yes |
| LoRA + norm | 49 MB | 17 min | Strong, about level with LoRA | Yes |
| DoRA | 49 MB | 28 min | Strong | Yes |
| Norm-only, baked, `sd-cli` | 2.5 MB + a full checkpoint | 16 min | Beard and glasses; misses at a distance | Yes, mildly |

- Textual Inversion wins here. It trains one new word in both text encoders and leaves the
  drawing model alone, so without the word the model is unchanged. It's slower only because the text
  encoders run on the CPU to fit in 8 GB, and it's a little less stable: one astronaut came out as an
  illustration, one lost the face.
- The LoRA leak has a cheap fix. Training also on 20 plain pictures of "a man" made by the
  untouched model ([`make_reg.py`](../bench/imagegen/make_reg.py)) stopped the blue shirt and glasses
  turning up in plain prompts, at no visible cost to likeness on the beach and chef prompts. So
  the file is what's left to choose between: 8 KB, or 47 MB and half the training time.
- Fewer steps may do. With LoRA + norm, 250 steps (about 4 minutes) already showed the glasses
  and beard, and 500 looked close to 1000.
- Both file types work in `sd-cli`: our LoRA loads as it is (1120 of 1120 tensors), and
  [`bake_sdxl.py`](../bench/imagegen/bake_sdxl.py) writes norm-only into a checkpoint. A learned
  word goes in with `--embd-dir`.

### Words on styles, and stacking

The same kind of 8 KB word, on Van Gogh and Gris. It took 35 minutes each, through `sd-cli`, judged by
eye on two seeds. A 3000-step Van Gogh LoRA (50 minutes) is the comparison.

![Three rows of the same prompts: Van Gogh LoRA at 1000 steps, at 3000 steps, and the 8 KB learned word; the last column has no trigger word](image-style-word-vs-steps.jpg)

*Seed 1. The last column has no trigger word, so a changed picture there is a leak.*

| Van Gogh | File | Training | Look | Leaks? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Named in the prompt | none | none | Clear, swirling strokes | n/a |
| LoRA, 1000 steps | 47 MB | 17 min | Paint and palette, muted | Yes, darkens |
| **LoRA, 3000 steps** | 47 MB | 50 min | **About level with the prompt** | **Yes, strongly: a plain prompt comes out painted** |
| **Word** | **8 KB** | 35 min | Bold strokes, stronger than 1000 steps of LoRA, but everything turns green | **No** |

- Steps mattered more than we'd assumed. The earlier verdict that the prompt beats training was
  a verdict on 1000 steps. Three times as long closes the gap on Van Gogh, at the price of the worst
  leak we've seen. Regularisation pictures fixed the person's leak, but we haven't tried them on a style.
- The word has a colour habit. Van Gogh and Gris both came out green, in pictures of a cat and a
  street as well as the green coat. It isn't the starting word: starting a Van Gogh word from "art"
  instead of "painting" came out greener still. Mean green over the pictures went from 133 to 150, on
  10 of the 12 pictures (the scoring run hit its time limit). The cause is still unknown.
- Gris didn't work as cubism. The word gives an old oil look, stronger than LoRA, with no broken
  planes. Neither method learned the shapes, so only naming him gets them. Structure seems much
  harder to teach than paint.
- Two words stack. [`stack.py`](../bench/imagegen/stack.py) put `vgword style, zxqtok man` in one
  prompt, and the invented man kept his beard and glasses in Van Gogh's paint, though he also drifts
  towards Van Gogh's self-portraits. With Gris the man stayed and the style mostly went, so stacking is
  only as strong as the weaker word. All 16 pictures took about 12 minutes. Turning the style word
  down to 0.7 with `(word:0.7)` barely changed anything, so it's not a usable strength control.

```bash
mamba run -n vlft python bench/imagegen/train_style.py preflight --set subject --method norm --trigger zxq
mamba run -n vlft python bench/imagegen/invert.py --set subject --word zxqtok
mamba run -n vlft python bench/imagegen/eval_sdcli.py --run subject-word --trigger zxqtok
```

**A bug to avoid:** an SDXL VAE loaded on its own in diffusers gets SD 1.5's scaling factor, 0.18215
instead of 0.13025. Pictures come out off-colour and training quietly learns the wrong thing. It voided
our first full round of runs, so `train_style.py` now sets it explicitly.

Everything else on this page, flag for flag, is in [Reproducing our results](reproduce.md#pictures).

## What's next

Automatic style and likeness scores, a style the prompt can't name, and LoRA on Klein. See [Faster and better fine-tuning](up-next.md#faster-and-better-fine-tuning).
# 🏋️ pibench — six chores, nineteen models, one very warm laptop

Published benchmarks are run by the people who made the model, on big GPUs, at full precision,
with their own scaffolding. We wanted to know something smaller and more personal: **on this
laptop, at the quantization we actually run, through the agent we actually use, can the model
get a job done on its own?**

So we gave every model the same six chores, let it work alone, and graded the results with
tests it never saw.

## The chores

| Task | The job | Graded by |
|---|---|---|
| 🐛 **bug fix** | Two bug reports for a duration parser (`"2h5m"` → seconds). There are more bugs than reported. | 27 hidden tests, including the unreported bugs |
| 🧩 **feature** | Add error handling, a restock report and a CSV round trip to a small `Inventory` class | 8 tests, with commas, quotes and accents in the CSV |
| 🛠️ **CLI** | Write a word-frequency command-line tool from a spec (options, stdin, JSON out, exit codes) | 6 tests against a reference implementation |
| 📚 **books** | Scrape one category of [books.toscrape.com](https://books.toscrape.com), visiting every book's page for stock and UPC | 5 tests: titles, prices, ratings, stock, UPCs |
| 🪄 **JS page** | Scrape three pages of a site whose quotes are drawn by JavaScript | 5 tests: 30 quotes, authors, texts, tags, order |
| 🔐 **login** | Log in through a form with a CSRF token, then grab links that only logged-in users see | 3 tests: 15 authors and their exact links |

The web chores use [toscrape.com](https://toscrape.com), sandbox sites built for scraping
practice, so the answers don't change from one day to the next. Every task was checked both
ways before any model touched it: a reference solution passes every hidden test, and the
untouched starter files fail them.

**The rules:** plain [pi](https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono) 1.3.0 with no extensions, each
model at its launcher's defaults unless its page says otherwise (Gemma and LFM3-VL ran at 64K, the Gemma 12B agentic
at 32K on our fork), 20 minutes per attempt, **3 attempts per task**. An attempt
counts as **solved only if every hidden test passes**. Close doesn't count; it's that kind of
gym.

## The sandbox (because we let robots run shell commands)

Each attempt runs in a fresh Docker container that holds pi, Python, Playwright and a copy of
that task's files, and nothing else from the laptop. The model server stays on the host GPU,
reachable through a private Docker bridge.

```
host:       llama-server on the GPU, bound to the private bridge only (not the LAN)
container:  pi + Python + Playwright/Chromium · non-root · no capabilities · 4 GB RAM · 4 CPUs
network:    internet ✔   model server ✔   your LAN, router, other host ports ✘
```

A firewall rule blocks the LAN, and the runner refuses to start until a probe from inside the
sandbox confirms it can't reach the laptop's other addresses. Grading happens in a second
container with no network at all. The nuts and bolts (and how to run it yourself) are in
[`bench/README.md`](../bench/README.md).

